            "intro": {
              "type": "_text",
              "settings": {
                "eyebrow": "2025 — Stoneware",
                "heading": "Desert Totem",
                "heading_level": "h1",
                "text": "<p>Fired stoneware holds time differently than almost any other material. Civilizations disappear. Languages go silent. But the pottery stays — dug out of desert floors thousands of years later, still legible, still whole.</p><p>Desert Totem was built with that in mind. The forms are stacked, modular, deliberately incomplete-feeling — as if they were found rather than made. The surfaces are carved and glazed to read like something uncovered, not finished. Like it had already survived.</p><p>The work asks a simple question: if this piece were buried today and pulled from the ground in three thousand years, what would it say about what was made here?</p>",
                "style": "paragraph",
                "alignment": "left"
              }
            },
